    id: 'blog-28',
    title: 'Sweater Trends for Fall 2026: What to Wear and What to Skip',
    excerpt: 'From cropped cardigans to elevated basics, here are the knitwear trends worth investing in this season — and the ones to leave on the rack.',
    content: 'Every fall, a fresh wave of knitwear trends appears. Some are worth your money, others are fleeting. Here is an honest look at the fall 2026 sweater trends, with my take on which to embrace and which to skip.\n\n## Worth Investing In\n\n### Elevated Neutrals\n\nForget loud prints. This season is all about refined, tonal neutrals — cream, camel, oatmeal, soft grey. The focus is on beautiful fabric and clean lines rather than flashy patterns.\n\n**Why it works:** Neutral knits are endlessly versatile. They mix with everything, elevate any outfit, and never go out of style. This is the safest investment you can make.\n\n### The Fine-Gauge Turtleneck\n\nA slim, fine-gauge turtleneck in a neutral color is having a major moment. It is sleek, flattering, and perfect for layering under blazers and coats.\n\n**Why it works:** It is both stylish and practical. It reads as elegant while keeping you warm, and it layers effortlessly.\n\n### Texture Over Pattern\n\nCable knits, ribbed textures, and waffle stitches are trending. Designers are playing with tactile surfaces rather than loud prints, which adds visual interest in a subtle, sophisticated way.\n\n**Why it works:** Texture adds dimension without being loud. It photographs beautifully and feels premium.\n\n## Worth Trying (But Be Selective)\n\n### The Hooded Knit\n\nHooded knitwear — cardigans and sweaters with attached hoods — has been trending. It is cozy and casual, perfect for off-duty weekends.\n\n**My take:** Great for a relaxed piece, but buy it as a casual cardigan, not a dressy sweater. It does not translate to office or formal settings.\n\n### Color-Block and Vintage Patterns\n\nPlaid, retro color-blocking, and 1970s-inspired patterns are making a comeback. These pieces add personality and make great statement items.\n\n**My take:** Fun to own one or two, but they date quickly. Buy them cheap, and do not build your whole wardrobe around them.\n\n## Worth Skipping\n\n### Ultra-Oversized Everything\n\nThere was a trend toward extremely oversized, shapeless sweaters. While cozy, they can look sloppy and overwhelm your frame.\n\n**My take:** A relaxed fit is good, but choose pieces that still have some structure. You want cozy, not a potato sack.\n\n### Fast-Fashion Cashmere\n\nCheap "cashmere" from fast-fashion brands is trending because it is affordable. But most of it is low-grade, short-fiber cashmere that pills and falls apart after a few washes.\n\n**My take:** Avoid. A good cashmere blend or merino sweater will outlast and out-perform cheap pure cashmere.\n\n## How to Build Your Fall 2026 Knitwear Capsule\n\nBased on these trends, here is a focused capsule that gets the most value:\n\n1. **Cream fine-gauge turtleneck** — your versatile base\n2. **Camel crewneck sweater** — your everyday staple\n3. **Ribbed or cable texture piece** — your visual interest\n4. **A cozy hooded or open cardigan** — your casual layer\n5. **One accent color** (burgundy, forest green, rust) — your statement\n\nFive pieces, endless outfits. That is the smart way to engage with trends — take what serves you, skip what does not.\n\nAt LINFAIR, our collection focuses on elevated neutrals, fine-gauge classics, and cozy textures — the trends we believe will look as good next year as they do this fall.',
